architecture-the-glory-guide = THE GLORY GUIDE
architecture-architecture-and-design = Architecture and design.
architecture-glory-retains-a-typed-semantic-tree-builds-each-mounted = Glory retains a typed semantic tree, builds each mounted widget once, and sends reactive changes directly to the affected nodes. The same application model can be interpreted by browser, server, test, and native hosts.
architecture-a-retained-tree-not-a-virtual-dom = A retained tree, not a virtual DOM
architecture-mounting-a-widget-creates-semantic-nodes-with-stable-identities = Mounting a widget creates semantic nodes with stable identities and typed properties. A View, Text, Button, Input, Check, Image, Link, or Group records what the interface is; it does not ask the shared UI layer to create a DOM element or a native control. The active renderer retains those nodes and translates operations for its host.
architecture-widget-build-runs-once-for-each-mount-it-creates = Widget::build runs once for each mount. It creates the initial structure, allocates state in the supplied Scope, and installs event handlers and reactive bindings. A later state change does not call build again and does not manufacture a second tree to compare with the first. Builders use &mut self because mounting can consume their stored children and declarations.
architecture-in-this-example-count-map-creates-a-tracked-derived = In this example, count.map creates a tracked derived value and text().content installs a binding for it. The button handler revises the Cage. The runtime then wakes the affected binding, which updates the existing Text node. Constructing a new Counter value would describe a new mount; it is not part of the update path.
architecture-a-bare-build-time-read-is-not-reactive = A bare build-time read is not reactive
architecture-text-content-count-get-to-string-copies-one-value = text().content(count.get().to_string()) copies one value into a static property. Use count.map(...), another Bond, or content_fn(...) when the text must change. Reactive reads are tracked inside Bond, Binding, and Effect computations, not by an arbitrary read during build.
architecture-from-a-cage-revision-to-a-typed-patch = From a Cage revision to a typed Patch
architecture-cage-is-the-owned-mutable-source-it-is-not = Cage is the owned mutable source; it is not a diff engine. revise changes the source and schedules direct dependents. A text binding that observes the new value prepares glory::render::Patch::Text(Change::Set(new_text)) for its known node. Other properties use their own variants, such as Patch::Label, Patch::Classes, Patch::Style, Patch::Value, and Patch::Checked.
architecture-that-distinction-matters-for-both-correctness-and-performance-the = That distinction matters for both correctness and performance. The update already knows its target and its field, so no VDOM walk is needed to discover what changed. Typed patches also let a renderer validate revisions, controlled-input state, supported properties, and host capabilities before applying an operation. Writes are scheduled work: the runtime's batching and the host's event or frame boundary determine when the binding and renderer commit run.
architecture-this-does-not-make-every-computation-free-a-derived = This does not make every computation free. A derived computation still executes the Rust code you wrote, and a broad dependency can still cause unnecessary work. Keep derived values focused, use equality gates where they help, and use keyed each or the windowed list controller for collections rather than rebuilding an entire screen from a Cage.
architecture-change-structure-with-control-flow = Change structure with control flow
architecture-property-bindings-cannot-add-or-remove-semantic-nodes-use = Property bindings cannot add or remove semantic nodes. Use when for conditional branches, each for ordinary keyed collections, and list for large windowed collections. These controllers own the mount lifetime of their branches and rows. Source reads performed by structural factories are handled separately from the pure property bindings.
architecture-group-is-logical-view-has-a-box = Group is logical; View has a box
architecture-use-group-when-children-need-a-logical-parent-but = Use group() when children need a logical parent but no physical wrapper. Group has no layout box, no implicit accessibility role, and no physical geometry or scrolling target. It can still participate in logical ancestry and appropriate logical event routing. This makes it useful for returning several children without distorting a flex or grid layout.
architecture-use-view-when-you-need-a-layout-box-typed = Use view() when you need a layout box, typed styles, classes, or a structural document role. row() and col() are View constructors with flex direction defaults, not special renderer primitives. Do not put a Group into a layout calculation and expect it to have dimensions; query a boxed descendant or use a View instead.
architecture-owner-is-the-lifetime-boundary = Owner is the lifetime boundary
architecture-every-ui-scope-wraps-a-core-owner-and-adds = Every UI Scope wraps a core Owner and adds semantic construction context. Cages, Bonds, bindings, effects, listeners, tasks, and mounted children created through that scope belong to its ownership tree. show_in and fill retain the resulting mounts in their parent. When control flow removes a branch or an application drops a mount, disposing its Owner retires that subtree and cancels its owned work.
architecture-a-cage-is-a-copy-generational-handle-not-an = A Cage is a Copy, generational handle, not an Rc-like lifetime extension. Capturing it in a callback does not keep its Owner alive; operations on a retired handle fail rather than addressing a reused value. Owner::on_cleanup registers explicit cleanup, with children cleaned before parents. Prefer scope.downgrade() when a long-lived callback needs an optional Scope reference instead of strongly retaining the UI scope.
architecture-ownership-also-explains-independent-component-state-two-mounts-of = Ownership also explains independent component state. Two mounts of Counter execute build twice in two child scopes and allocate two distinct count Cages. They may share application state only when you deliberately capture a Cage from a longer-lived owner. Normal fill and show_in calls manage child scopes for you; manually created child UI scopes must be retained for as long as their mounted UI should live.
architecture-review-cage-bond-and-lotus-ownership = Review Cage, Bond, and Lotus ownership  →
architecture-one-semantic-model-replaceable-hosts = One semantic model, replaceable hosts
architecture-the-shared-widget-describes-semantics-and-behavior-while-the = The shared widget describes semantics and behavior, while the host supplies presentation and execution services. The web feature interprets semantic operations through the browser DOM and browser input. The ssr feature synchronously serializes HTML or versioned Snapshot fragments; event handlers do not become executable JavaScript. The optional Blitz host interprets the same semantic operations with native CSS layout, text, painting, and desktop window integration.
architecture-app-memory-provides-a-reference-interpreter-for-semantic-tests = App::memory provides a reference interpreter for semantic tests. It can exercise mounts, patches, and typed events without claiming to produce browser or native pixels. At the other boundary, WebApp and the Blitz desktop Shell keep their respective hosts alive and drive input, flushing, frames, and cleanup.
architecture-portability-is-a-semantic-and-typed-style-contract-not = Portability is a semantic and typed-style contract, not a promise of identical pixels on every engine. Check each host's supported style profile and interaction capabilities. Keep DOM lookup, HTTP serving, window creation, and other platform concerns in the host entry points so the shared widget remains reusable.
architecture-the-mental-model = The mental model
architecture-build-structure-once-bind-changing-properties-explicitly-let-control = Build structure once, bind changing properties explicitly, let control flow own structural changes, and let the selected host interpret typed semantic operations. There is no hidden render-function rerun or VDOM reconciliation pass between a Cage revision and a Patch.
custom-widgets-writing-custom-widgets = Writing custom widgets.
custom-widgets-implementing-widget-gives-a-reusable-rust-value-its-own = Implementing Widget gives a reusable Rust value its own construction logic, state ownership, children, and semantic event handlers. The trait is small; understanding build, Scope, and mount lifetime is what makes the component correct.
custom-widgets-a-complete-counter = A complete Counter
custom-widgets-the-following-component-is-host-independent-it-contains-no = The following component is host-independent. It contains no DOM types, browser listeners, HTML strings, or native window calls. Mount Counter through Web, SSR, Blitz, or the memory host and the same semantic tree and event behavior are used.
custom-widgets-the-required-method-is-fn-build-mut-self-scope = The required method is fn build(&mut self, scope: &Scope) -> Result. Result here is Glory's UI result alias, so node creation, child mounting, and other fallible operations can use ?. The final col().show_in(scope) mounts the root builder into the Counter's scope and returns that Result. Returning the bare col() would be a type error inside a Widget implementation.
custom-widgets-state-belongs-to-the-mounted-scope = State belongs to the mounted Scope
custom-widgets-scope-cage-0-i32-creates-mutable-state-owned-by = scope.cage(0_i32) creates mutable state owned by this mount. The returned Cage is a small Copy handle, so it can be captured by the derived label and the button handler. There is no count field on Counter: the state is runtime-owned and lives for exactly as long as the mounted Scope. Every separate Counter mount gets an independent zero.
custom-widgets-count-map-derives-a-reactive-string-for-text-content = count.map derives a reactive String for text().content. That builder method accepts values convertible into Lotus, allowing either a static String or a reactive source. When the event handler calls revise, the derived value and text binding are scheduled; build is not called again. Avoid creating maps or other reactive nodes inside pure derived computations.
custom-widgets-build-is-construction-not-a-render-function = Build is construction, not a render function
custom-widgets-do-not-expect-self-fields-or-a-bare-cage = Do not expect self fields or a bare Cage read to refresh the interface. Store configuration in self, create owned reactive state in build, and install bindings for every property that must change.
custom-widgets-activate-is-a-semantic-event = Activate is a semantic Event
custom-widgets-on-activate-handler-subscribes-to-eventkind-activate-the-callback = .on(activate, handler) subscribes to EventKind::Activate. The callback receives a typed glory::render::Event, re-exported by the prelude. Event::Activate represents a control activation request without making the shared widget depend on a browser MouseEvent or keyboard event. The host handles native input and queues the semantic event; Glory routes it to the registered handler.
custom-widgets-the-matches-check-in-counter-is-explicit-about-the = The matches! check in Counter is explicit about the payload, although a handler registered only for activate will normally receive Event::Activate. Other controls use other event kinds and payloads: value_changed carries text and a revision, while checked_changed carries a boolean. Do not simulate input by directly invoking DOM events from shared component logic; drive application state or use semantic events in tests.
custom-widgets-fill-accepts-intochildren = fill accepts IntoChildren
custom-widgets-view-group-button-and-link-builders-accept-children-through = View, Group, Button, and Link builders accept children through fill. The IntoChildren implementations cover one Widget, tuples of heterogeneous widgets, Vec<W>, and () for no children. A tuple is the usual choice for a fixed layout. Use Vec<Box<dyn Widget>> when a single heterogeneous vector is more convenient, and use each or list when children represent a reactive collection.
custom-widgets-a-reusable-wrapper-can-accept-impl-intochildren-in-its = A reusable wrapper can accept impl IntoChildren in its constructor and store the resulting glory::ui::Children. Children is a consumed mount input, not a cloneable template. Because build receives &mut self and runs once, replace it with an empty child list and pass the original value to fill. fill then mounts each child beneath the new semantic parent.
custom-widgets-panel-new-heading-body-counter-works-because-the-three = Panel::new((heading, body, Counter)) works because the three-element tuple implements IntoChildren. Calling fill more than once extends the builder's stored child list; it does not replace earlier children. This differs from class and style, which configure one replaceable property slot.
custom-widgets-calling-class-twice-replaces-the-complete-whitespace-separated-class = Calling .class(...) twice replaces the complete whitespace-separated class list on that builder; it does not append. Similarly, .style(...) replaces the complete typed Style value and removes any earlier binding for that property slot. This rule applies whether the new value is static or reactive. CSS cascade rules can still combine styles from external stylesheets, but two builder calls are not a merge API.
custom-widgets-construct-one-style-with-chained-field-methods-when-you = Construct one Style with chained field methods when you want to combine fields. Be especially careful with row() and col(): their initial flex display and direction are just a Style value. Supplying a replacement style without those fields removes the convenience defaults. Start from view(), or include display and flex_direction explicitly in the replacement.
custom-widgets-children-and-owner-are-linked-not-identical = Children and Owner are linked, not identical
custom-widgets-semantic-parentage-and-reactive-ownership-are-related-but-distinct = Semantic parentage and reactive ownership are related but distinct. The Panel's View is the semantic parent of its children. Each child passed through fill is mounted in a fresh child Scope, and the constructing scope retains that mount. The child Owner therefore follows the Panel's lifetime even though the child can have its own state, subscriptions, tasks, and descendants.
custom-widgets-removing-a-mounted-branch-disposes-its-owner-and-descendants = Removing a mounted branch disposes its Owner and descendants, invalidates their generational handles, removes listeners and semantic nodes, and cancels owned queued work. Capturing a child's Cage in a parent callback does not invert that relationship or keep the child alive. Pass application state down deliberately from a longer-lived Scope when it must outlive a branch.
custom-widgets-you-normally-do-not-need-to-call-scope-child = You normally do not need to call scope.child() yourself: fill, show_in, mount_with, and control-flow controllers establish and retain the required child scopes. If a custom controller creates a child UI Scope directly, it must retain that Scope or its Mounted guard for the entire mounted lifetime. Dropping the guard disposes the subtree.
custom-widgets-choose-a-trait-closure-or-plain-function = Choose a trait, closure, or plain function
custom-widgets-implement-widget-when-the-component-has-a-reusable-name = Implement Widget when the component has a reusable name, configuration, accepted children, or trait-based composition. Counter and Panel are good examples. Use widget(|scope| ...) when a local component needs owned state but does not justify a named struct. The closure must implement FnMut(&Scope) -> Result + 'static and is still built only once per mount.
custom-widgets-a-plain-fn-card-impl-widget-is-enough-for = A plain fn card(...) -> impl Widget is enough for stateless composition or for returning another widget configured with arguments. If that function needs a Scope to allocate local Cages, return widget(...) rather than trying to read state during ordinary function execution. None of these choices changes the update model: dynamic properties require bindings, and structural changes require control flow.
custom-widgets-review-the-retained-tree-and-update-model = Review the retained tree and update model  →
custom-widgets-a-mount-checklist = A mount checklist
custom-widgets-allocate-state-from-the-build-scope-finish-the-root = Allocate state from the build Scope; finish the root with show_in(scope); consume stored children exactly once; use typed Event handlers; and let fill, show_in, or an explicit Mounted guard retain every child lifetime.
