platforms-browser-adoption-of-a-compatible-ssr-snapshot-through-hydrate = Browser adoption of a compatible SSR snapshot through hydrate or hydrate_with. It includes web, validates before changing the DOM, and preserves the mount on mismatch.
platforms-streaming-stream = Streaming · stream
platforms-native-pull-driven-shell-patch-end-output-with-request = Native pull-driven Shell/Patch/End output with request resources and cancellation. Add web for the classic pre-edit script and hydrate for final application ownership; streaming alone is not a browser input host.
platforms-blitz-document-blitz = Blitz document · blitz
platforms-native-headless-css-box-text-layout-measurement-and-programmatic = Native headless CSS/box/text layout, measurement, and programmatic scrolling. Optional paint/raster adds frames or CPU images. A standalone Renderer has no OS input driver.
platforms-blitz-desktop-blitz-shell = Blitz desktop · blitz-shell
platforms-windowapp-and-shell-add-cpu-desktop-windows-activation-focus = WindowApp and Shell add CPU desktop windows, activation, focus, navigation, controlled editing, and IME protocol integration on the desktop targets. AccessKit is a separate opt-in.
platforms-sharing-semantics-does-not-erase-platform-differences = Sharing semantics does not erase platform differences
platforms-cross-platform-widgets-do-not-guarantee-pixel-identical-output = Cross-platform widgets do not guarantee pixel-identical output. Fonts, shaping, viewport/DPI behavior, CSS coverage, and native presentation differ. A green headless test proves neither layout nor displayed pixels, and neither headless memory nor a standalone Blitz Renderer supplies user input.
platforms-keep-current-platform-limits-explicit = Keep current platform limits explicit
platforms-the-desktop-shell-is-gated-to-windows-linux-and = The desktop shell is gated to Windows, Linux, and macOS; it is not an Android host. Android Activity/Surface integration, mobile resources, input connections, and device acceptance remain separate work. Do not infer mobile support from an Android compile check or from desktop winit dependencies.
platforms-ime-protocol-handling-is-not-proof-that-every-operating = IME protocol handling is not proof that every operating-system IME works. Likewise, the optional AccessKit bridge and Windows UI Automation evidence are not screen-reader acceptance. Unix AT-SPI client checks, spoken announcements, complete text-selection interfaces, OS IME behavior, and mobile accessibility still require platform-specific validation.
reactivity-state-updates = STATE & UPDATES
reactivity-fine-grained-reactivity = Fine-grained reactivity.
reactivity-cage-bond-and-lotus-make-data-flow-explicit-values = Cage, Bond, and Lotus make data flow explicit. Values and subscriptions belong to an Owner, so their lifetime stays connected to the widget tree.
reactivity-an-owned-copy-handle-to-mutable-application-state-revise = An owned, Copy handle to mutable application state. Revise it to notify dependent computations.
reactivity-a-tracked-derived-value-equality-gates-are-opt-in = A tracked derived value. Equality gates are opt-in and can avoid redundant downstream work.
reactivity-a-uniform-value-input-that-lets-widget-builders-accept = A uniform value input that lets widget builders accept static or reactive content.
reactivity-derived-values-without-the-mystery = Derived values without the mystery
reactivity-reads-inside-bond-binding-and-effect-computations-are-tracked = Reads inside Bond, binding, and effect computations are tracked. A bare read while constructing a widget is not a subscription.
reactivity-try-a-live-widget = Try a live widget
reactivity-this-counter-is-running-through-glory-s-browser-host = This counter is running through Glory's browser host. Its label derives directly from the same Cage used by the event handlers.
reactivity-ownership-is-part-of-the-model = Ownership is part of the model
reactivity-dropping-a-scope-invalidates-its-handles-and-cancels-owned = Dropping a scope invalidates its handles and cancels owned work. Collection projections and indexed lists preserve precise dependencies without scanning an entire data source.
ssr-server-rendering-adoption = SERVER RENDERING & ADOPTION
ssr-ssr-and-hydration = SSR and hydration.
ssr-render-useful-html-on-the-server-then-let-the = Render useful HTML on the server, then let the browser adopt that exact semantic tree. Glory keeps static output, snapshots, resource transfer, and progressive streaming as explicit choices.
ssr-start-with-ordinary-html = Start with ordinary HTML
ssr-use-ssr-render-for-a-fragment-and-ssr-render = Use ssr::render for a fragment and ssr::render_document for a complete document with metadata, stylesheets, and BASE_CSS. These APIs produce ordinary server-rendered HTML, not a versioned hydration Snapshot. There is no hydration envelope for web::hydrate to adopt.
ssr-plain-html-is-not-hydratable = Plain HTML is not hydratable
ssr-choose-render-or-render-document-for-static-pages-do = Choose render or render_document for static pages. Do not place their output in a mount and call hydrate: adoption requires snapshot or snapshot_with (or a completed stream).
ssr-create-an-adoptable-snapshot = Create an adoptable snapshot
ssr-snapshot-and-snapshot-with-wrap-the-mapper-output-in = snapshot and snapshot_with wrap the mapper output in Glory's versioned fragment boundaries. Insert Snapshot::html verbatim into an otherwise empty, exclusively owned mount element. Include glory::html::BASE_CSS in the document; unlike render_document, manual snapshot assembly does not add it for you.
ssr-hydrate-the-same-application = Hydrate the same application
ssr-in-the-wasm32-browser-host-call-web-hydrate-with = In the wasm32 browser host, call web::hydrate with a widget or web::hydrate_with with a factory. The factory, structure, and initial data must match the server. Hydration validates the entire subtree before attaching runtime state; a version or structure mismatch returns HydrationError and preserves the existing DOM.
ssr-synchronous-ssr-does-not-await = Synchronous SSR does not await
ssr-render-snapshot-and-their-with-forms-construct-and-serialize = render, snapshot, and their _with forms construct and serialize the current tree immediately. They do not block on async Resource loaders or wait for pending values. Seed known terminal data explicitly, render a pending state, or use stream_with when the server should progressively await work.
ssr-transfer-resources-explicitly = Transfer resources explicitly
ssr-the-optional-resources-feature-transfers-only-live-named-terminal = The optional resources feature transfers only live, named terminal values. Give every binding a stable name and schema string, and make its business key explicit. For synchronous SSR, prefetch into a resource Snapshot with seed, restore a fresh Resources context, render with snapshot_resources_with, and export before App cleanup removes registrations.
ssr-resource-script-creates-an-escaped-inert-application-json-block = resource_script creates an escaped, inert application/json block, not executable bootstrap code. Put it outside the hydrated mount. In the browser, hydrate_resources_with reads it with explicit Limits, restores Resources, and runs the same named bindings. A matching success or error seed skips only the initial request.
ssr-one-context-per-request = One context per request
ssr-never-share-a-resources-context-between-requests-or-applications = Never share a Resources context between requests or applications; clones share that context. Create a new one every time. On a failed low-level adoption attempt, restore a fresh context from the original payload for retry. hydrate_resources_with preserves the data block on failure and removes it only after success.
ssr-stream-shell-patch-and-end = Stream Shell, Patch, and End
ssr-with-the-native-stream-feature-ssr-stream-with-returns = With the native stream feature, ssr::stream_with returns a local, pull-driven request body. Shell is available before loader polling, Patch frames carry semantic HTML edits as work completes, and End carries terminal resources plus canonical key relabeling. Serve web::STREAM_SCRIPT under your CSP and emit Bootstrap::head_script before the Shell so early input is captured.
ssr-backpressure-is-the-polling-contract-request-the-next-frame = Backpressure is the polling contract: request the next frame only after the previous chunk has been written. Pausing demand pauses rendering and loader polling; dropping the body cancels its App and futures. Do not replace this with an eager producer that buffers the whole response.
ssr-wait-for-the-final-state = Wait for the final state
ssr-a-streaming-shell-uses-temporary-stream-markers-and-is = A streaming Shell uses temporary stream markers and is deliberately not adoptable. Never hydrate the Shell or a mid-stream Patch. After End has been applied, the root dispatches glory:stream-ready; only then hydrate the finalized tree with the same factory and transferred resources.
ssr-choose-the-rendering-path = Choose the rendering path
ssr-need = Need
ssr-server-api = Server API
ssr-browser-outcome = Browser outcome
ssr-static-content = Static content
ssr-render-render-document = render / render_document
ssr-plain-html-no-adoption = Plain HTML; no adoption
ssr-interactive-initial-tree = Interactive initial tree
ssr-snapshot-snapshot-with = snapshot / snapshot_with
ssr-hydrate-hydrate-with = hydrate / hydrate_with
ssr-prefetched-named-data = Prefetched named data
ssr-snapshot-resources-with-resource-script = snapshot_resources_with + resource_script
ssr-progressive-async-work = Progressive async work
ssr-stream-with-bootstrap = stream_with + Bootstrap
ssr-hydrate-only-after-end-glory-stream-ready = Hydrate only after End / glory:stream-ready
ssr-common-pitfalls = Common pitfalls
ssr-rebuilding-instead-of-adopting = Rebuilding instead of adopting
ssr-launch-requires-an-empty-mount-and-creates-a-new = launch requires an empty mount and creates a new tree. Use hydrate for a Snapshot; do not clear or rewrite server markup first.
ssr-changing-initial-inputs = Changing initial inputs
ssr-a-different-factory-initial-cage-value-resource-name-schema = A different factory, initial Cage value, resource name, schema, or final resource state can produce a mismatch or an extra load. Share the component and seed contract.
ssr-dropping-webapp = Dropping WebApp
ssr-the-returned-webapp-is-a-lifetime-guard-not-a = The returned WebApp is a lifetime guard, not a throwaway result. Store it for as long as the interface should remain mounted and interactive.
ssr-expecting-implicit-async-ssr = Expecting implicit async SSR
ssr-a-synchronous-snapshot-never-waits-for-a-future-use = A synchronous snapshot never waits for a future. Use explicit seeds for already known values, or the pull-driven stream for progressive completion.
ssr-nesting-the-data-block = Nesting the data block
ssr-keep-resource-script-output-outside-the-mount-hydration-owns = Keep resource_script output outside the mount. Hydration owns the mount's complete subtree; the separate JSON block has its own identity and lifetime.
ssr-bring-your-own-production-http-server = Bring your own production HTTP server
ssr-glory-supplies-rendering-frames-and-browser-bootstrap-assets-not = Glory supplies rendering, frames, and browser bootstrap assets—not a production HTTP server. Routing, TLS, concurrency, socket writes, CSP, disconnect handling, and deployment belong to your host. The semantic-web ssr_fixture and stream_fixture binaries are acceptance fixtures, not Axum or Salvo integrations and not production server implementations.
styling-style-presentation = STYLE & PRESENTATION
styling-style-without-losing-the-boundaries = Style without losing the boundaries.
styling-choose-typed-style-values-for-portable-declarations-class-names = Choose typed Style values for portable declarations, class names for document CSS, or a validated Sheet for a bounded portable subset. The rendering host still owns CSS cascade, fonts, and layout.
styling-replacement-not-accumulation = Replacement, not accumulation
styling-style-replaces-the-complete-typed-style-and-class-replaces = style(...) replaces the complete typed Style and class(...) replaces the complete whitespace-separated class list. Repeated calls do not append or merge. Compute the final value first, including any layout declarations supplied by row() or col().
styling-use-typed-style-for-semantic-declarations = Use typed Style for semantic declarations
styling-style-has-explicit-rust-types-for-dimensions-flex-layout = Style has explicit Rust types for dimensions, flex layout, overflow, color, typography, borders, and other supported properties. Absent fields emit no inline declaration; explicit default values remain meaningful.
styling-use-classes-with-external-css = Use classes with external CSS
styling-classes-connect-semantic-nodes-to-the-surrounding-document-s = Classes connect semantic nodes to the surrounding document's CSS. External CSS is not parsed as a typed Style and can use the host engine's wider CSS syntax. Serve it from the browser document, list it in an SSR Document, or install it through the native host; Glory does not infer a shared stylesheet from a class name.
styling-parse-a-bounded-sheet = Parse a bounded Sheet
styling-enable-glory-s-css-feature-to-parse-an-immutable = Enable Glory's css feature to parse an immutable Sheet. It accepts the current typed properties, class-based child/descendant selectors, and hover/focus/enabled/disabled/checked states. At-rules, id/type selectors, unknown declarations, unsupported values, and inputs beyond its budgets are rejected. It is not a complete CSS parser.
styling-the-example-uses-ssr-css-render-document-checks-and = The example uses ssr + css: render_document checks and lowers every rule for Profile::HTML. For manual integration, glory::html::sheet(&sheet, profile) produces checked HTML-family CSS; a Blitz renderer with CSS support uses set_sheet(&sheet). The target profile is checked again before installation.
styling-distinguish-absent-sizes-from-unlimited-sizes = Distinguish absent sizes from unlimited sizes
styling-maximum-sizes-use-limit-not-length-option-none-means = Maximum sizes use Limit, not Length. Option::None means the field is absent and leaves the stylesheet in charge; Some(Limit::None) explicitly emits an unbounded maximum and removes a stylesheet cap. Length::Auto belongs to preferred and minimum sizes, not max_width or max_height.
styling-check-the-selected-host-profile = Check the selected host profile
styling-each-renderer-exposes-an-immutable-style-profile-for-the = Each renderer exposes an immutable style profile for the App lifetime. Mounting and typed style updates validate values and reject unsupported properties or units before committing them. Profile::RECORD accepts declarations without performing layout; HTML and Blitz have different real engine boundaries.
styling-portable-values-are-not-identical-pixels = Portable values are not identical pixels
styling-a-declaration-accepted-by-two-hosts-can-still-resolve = A declaration accepted by two hosts can still resolve through different fonts, text shaping, viewport rules, and CSS engines. Test important layouts on every target instead of expecting pixel-identical output.
web-web-applications = Web applications.
web-a-glory-browser-application-is-a-wasm32-rust-library = A Glory browser application is a wasm32 Rust library started by wasm-bindgen. The host entry point finds an empty web_sys::Element, mounts the shared widget tree, retains the WebApp guard, and serves the generated JavaScript and WASM over HTTP.
web-select-the-wasm32-web-feature = Select the wasm32 Web feature
web-enable-glory-s-web-feature-for-a-dom-application = Enable Glory's web feature for a DOM application. It selects the HTML mapper and browser host; the shared Counter itself still needs only glory::prelude::*. Add wasm-bindgen for the start export and web-sys with Window, Document, and Element for mount lookup. Keep the wasm-bindgen CLI used after compilation at the same version as the locked Rust crate.
web-the-cdylib-output-is-the-wasm-library-loaded-by = The cdylib output is the WASM library loaded by the browser; rlib keeps the code available to native tests and server-side rendering. Build the browser entry with --target wasm32-unknown-unknown. The old web-csr and web-ssr feature names are not part of this API: choose web, ssr, and optionally hydrate.
web-find-the-root-and-call-launch = Find the root and call launch
web-launch-root-widget-mounts-an-already-constructed-widget-into = launch(root, widget) mounts an already constructed Widget into a web_sys::Element. The complete entry point below uses the Counter from the custom-widget guide. Convert Glory's error into JsValue so module startup reports a useful failure instead of silently leaving an empty page.
web-webapp-is-a-lifetime-guard = WebApp is a lifetime guard
web-do-not-let-the-returned-webapp-fall-out-of = Do not let the returned WebApp fall out of scope at the end of start. Dropping it releases the DOM, observers, native listeners, animation frame, and owned async tasks. Store it in application state or a thread_local guard for as long as the interface should run.
web-use-launch-with-for-root-scope-construction = Use launch_with for root-scope construction
web-launch-with-root-factory-accepts-fnonce-scope-w-it = launch_with(root, factory) accepts FnOnce(&Scope) -> W. It is useful when constructing the root widget also needs to allocate Cages or other state in the root scope. The factory returns the widget; it does not call show_in itself. launch(root, widget) is the convenient equivalent when the widget value is already available.
web-both-functions-return-result-webapp-the-factory-form-is = Both functions return Result<WebApp>. The factory form is not a recurring render callback: it runs once during mount, exactly like Widget::build. Reactive values installed there update the retained tree through bindings after startup.
web-the-csr-mount-must-be-empty = The CSR mount must be empty
web-launch-and-launch-with-require-an-empty-unclaimed-root = launch and launch_with require an empty, unclaimed root. They reject an element with any child nodes or an existing data-gly-app attribute. Child text nodes count too, so keep whitespace, comments, fallback content, and server markup out of a CSR mount. This guard prevents client rendering from silently deleting content that should instead be hydrated.
web-the-module-imports-the-javascript-generated-by-wasm-bindgen = The module imports the JavaScript generated by wasm-bindgen and awaits initialization. The #[wasm_bindgen(start)] function then runs, finds #app, and mounts Glory. You do not need to create DOM elements in Rust startup beyond looking up this root; the semantic Web renderer creates and updates the application's DOM.
web-build-bindings-and-serve-over-http = Build bindings and serve over HTTP
web-compile-the-library-to-wasm-run-wasm-bindgen-with = Compile the library to WASM, run wasm-bindgen with --target web, and place index.html beside the generated .js and _bg.wasm files. Then start an HTTP server for that output directory and open the shown localhost URL in a browser.
web-do-not-open-index-html-with-file = Do not open index.html with file://
web-es-module-and-wasm-loading-require-a-proper-http = ES module and WASM loading require a proper HTTP origin and MIME handling; browser file:// security rules commonly block those requests. Serve the generated directory over HTTP even for local development. A successful native cargo build alone does not exercise this browser startup path.
web-link-ssr-to-the-browser-with-hydrate = Link SSR to the browser with hydrate
web-client-only-launch-creates-a-fresh-dom-hydration-is = Client-only launch creates a fresh DOM. Hydration is a separate, explicit operation that adopts a versioned SSR Snapshot without replaying initial property setters or replacing the existing subtree. Enable ssr in the native server build and hydrate in the wasm32 browser build; hydrate implies web. If one dependency declaration serves both targets, features = ["web", "ssr", "hydrate"] selects the complete setup.
web-put-the-shared-widget-factory-in-code-compiled-for = Put the shared widget factory in code compiled for both targets. The server and browser construct equivalent initial semantic trees from the same application logic. Arbitrary Rust heap state is not magically transferred by hydration; initial values must agree, and asynchronous request data needs an explicit resource or application data strategy.
web-serialize-a-snapshot-not-plain-markup = Serialize a Snapshot, not plain markup
web-glory-ssr-snapshot-app-returns-a-snapshot-whose-html = glory::ssr::snapshot(app()) returns a Snapshot whose html includes the version markers required by hydrate. Insert that fragment verbatim inside the exclusively owned #app element. Also include glory::html::BASE_CSS with the data-gly-base="v1" attribute; the browser host can then recognize the base style rather than duplicate it.
web-glory-ssr-render-is-appropriate-when-you-only-need = glory::ssr::render is appropriate when you only need HTML output, but its unversioned string is not the hydration contract. The Snapshot structure, widget factory, and browser feature set must agree. Hydration validates the complete initial subtree before attaching runtime metadata; on a version or structure mismatch it reports an error instead of repairing arbitrary DOM in place.
web-adopt-the-dom-and-retain-the-same-guard = Adopt the DOM and retain the same guard
web-use-hydrate-root-widget-or-hydrate-with-root-factory = Use hydrate(root, widget) or hydrate_with(root, factory) only for an SSR root. Do not call launch on that root first: launch requires emptiness, while hydrate expects the versioned server fragment. The browser still constructs the widget to establish state and bindings, but adopts matching DOM instead of recreating it. Existing eligible user input is preserved through the adoption rules.
web-the-hydrate-with-factory-has-the-same-fnonce-scope = The hydrate_with factory has the same FnOnce(&Scope) -> W shape as launch_with and is useful when the shared factory returns both a widget and handles needed by browser application state. Whichever entry point you use, retain the returned WebApp. Hydration changes how the initial DOM is acquired, not who owns the running app afterward.
web-review-getting-started-and-host-features = Review getting started and host features  →
web-the-complete-startup-contract = The complete startup contract
web-build-for-wasm32-with-web-look-up-a-web = Build for wasm32 with web; look up a web_sys root; use launch or launch_with only on an empty CSR mount; serve modules and WASM over HTTP; retain WebApp; and choose hydrate or hydrate_with with the matching SSR Snapshot when adopting server DOM.
widgets-the-semantic-toolbox = THE SEMANTIC TOOLBOX
widgets-compose-with-built-in-widgets = Compose with built-in widgets.
widgets-glory-s-built-ins-are-finite-semantic-building-blocks = Glory's built-ins are finite semantic building blocks. Write ordinary Rust builders, connect owned state, and let the selected host supply layout, input, and presentation.
widgets-know-what-is-built-in = Know what is built in
widgets-glory-provides-view-group-text-button-input-check-image = Glory provides view/group/text/button/input/check/image/link/file/select/graphic/rich_editor, plus row/col/when/each/list. Cards and tabs are Sapid widgets, not Glory built-ins. There is no textarea builder: use input().multiline(true).
widgets-import-the-builders = Import the builders
widgets-the-prelude-includes-the-common-builders-control-flow-reactive = The prelude includes the common builders, control flow, reactive values, events, and typed styles. Import file, select, graphic, and rich_editor explicitly from glory::ui; their configuration types live under glory::render.
widgets-structure-content-and-controls = Structure, content, and controls
widgets-structure = Structure
widgets-view-creates-a-styled-semantic-container-group-joins-logical = view creates a styled semantic container. group joins logical children without adding a layout box. row and col are flex-configured views.
widgets-content = Content
widgets-text-carries-inline-or-structured-text-image-loads-an = text carries inline or structured text, image loads an image with explicit alternative text, link targets a location, and graphic draws bounded vector paths.
widgets-input = Input
widgets-button-input-check-file-select-and-rich-editor-expose = button, input, check, file, select, and rich_editor expose semantic properties and typed events rather than DOM elements.
widgets-every-image-needs-alt-or-decorative-use-view-for = Every image needs alt(...) or decorative(). Use view for structural roles and physical queries; group is boxless. Link uses target(...), and controls accept static values or Lotus-compatible reactive handles.
widgets-files-selection-graphics-and-rich-text = Files, selection, graphics, and rich text
widgets-advanced-controls-use-bounded-configuration-values-select-options-have = Advanced controls use bounded configuration values. Select options have stable SemanticId keys, file pickers declare filters and budgets, graphics contain finite path commands, and rich editors consume a shared RichDocument.
widgets-choose-the-right-composition-tool = Choose the right composition tool
widgets-row-col = row / col
widgets-use-these-shortcuts-for-horizontal-or-vertical-flex-layout = Use these shortcuts for horizontal or vertical flex layout. A later style call replaces their prepared Style, so redeclare display and flex_direction when replacing it.
widgets-mount-a-branch-from-a-static-or-reactive-boolean = Mount a branch from a static or reactive boolean. otherwise supplies the alternate branch; changing branches disposes the old branch's owned work.
widgets-each-list = each / list
widgets-each-mounts-a-complete-keyed-vec-and-gives-rows = each mounts a complete keyed Vec and gives rows reactive item/index handles. list indexes a large source lazily and mounts only its visible window, overscan, and at most one focused row.
widgets-use-stable-keys-in-both-collection-builders-prefer-each = Use stable keys in both collection builders. Prefer each for small complete collections; use list with a bounded key lookup and fixed or measured row heights for large scrollable data.
counter-clicks = Clicks: { $count }
language-switch = 简体中文
language-switch-name = Switch to Simplified Chinese
